<h2>The 6 YouTube Channels Every Roofing Contractor Should Subscribe To</h2>

<p>YouTube is the second largest search engine in the world, and roofing content is exploding. These channels combine marketing education with business strategy.</p>

<h3>1. Roofing Insights — Dmitry Lipinskiy</h3>

<p>Dmitry Lipinskiy's Roofing Insights channel has approximately 183,000 subscribers, making it the largest roofing business channel on YouTube. Lipinskiy founded the channel in 2017 while running Storm Group Roofing, which he grew to over $5 million in annual revenue before selling.</p>

<p>The channel covers product reviews, industry investigations, contractor interviews, and business education. Lipinskiy is known for exposing scams and unethical practices, which builds massive trust with his audience. For contractors, Roofing Insights offers a masterclass in using controversy and investigative content to build a brand. Lipinskiy also runs Roofing Business School and the Directorii platform, which connects homeowners with vetted contractors.</p>

<h3>2. The Roof Strategist — Adam Bensman</h3>

<p>Adam Bensman's YouTube channel has approximately 72,000 subscribers and complements his podcast perfectly. The channel features sales role-plays, door-knocking techniques, and closing strategies. For marketing purposes, Bensman's content teaches contractors how to think like salespeople, which transforms how they write ad copy, design landing pages, and structure follow-up sequences.</p>

<h3>3. Roofer Growth Hacks — Chris Hunter</h3>

<p>Chris Hunter's YouTube channel extends his podcast content with visual demonstrations of marketing tactics. He covers SEO tutorials, Google Ads walkthroughs, and website reviews. Hunter's video content is particularly valuable for contractors who are visual learners and need to see exactly how to implement marketing strategies.</p>

<h3>4. Roofing SEO Podcast — Nolen Walker</h3>

<p>Nolen Walker's YouTube channel features video versions of his podcast episodes, plus screen recordings of SEO tools and website audits. Contractors can watch him analyze actual roofing websites, identify ranking problems, and recommend fixes. This hands-on approach makes complex SEO concepts accessible to non-technical business owners.</p>

<h3>5. Scrappy Wins / Minyona — Michael Mignogna</h3>

<p>Michael Mignogna's YouTube channel focuses on Facebook marketing, lead conversion, and sales strategies for roofing contractors. His content is practical and implementation-focused, with less theory and more "here is exactly what to do." For contractors spending money on Facebook ads, this channel is essential.</p>

<h3>6. RoofersCoffeeShop</h3>

<p>The RoofersCoffeeShop YouTube channel features industry news, technology updates, and interviews with roofing leaders. While less focused on marketing tactics than other channels, it provides the industry context that informs smart marketing decisions. Understanding where the industry is heading helps contractors position their messaging ahead of trends.</p>

<h3>Video Marketing Best Practices for Roofing Contractors</h3>

<p>These YouTube channels do more than educate. They model the exact video content that roofing contractors should be creating for their own marketing. Here is what the data shows:</p>

<ul>
  <li>85 percent of social video is watched without sound, so captions are non-negotiable.</li>
  <li>15 to 30 second shorts for TikTok, Instagram Reels, and YouTube Shorts generate the highest engagement.</li>
  <li>Before-and-after reveal videos, damage discovery clips, and time-lapse installations consistently outperform polished commercials.</li>
  <li>Contractors who film on actual job sites build more trust than those using stock footage.</li>
</ul>

